- How would you scale to 10+ variables with more operators and cycles?

## Why This Is Asked
To gauge practical reasoning about logic constraints, Horn-SAT familiarity, and ability to reason about linear-time algorithms and incremental updates in data pipelines.

## Key Concepts
- Horn clauses
- Forward chaining
- Partial vs full assignments
- Complexity O(n+m)
- Cycle handling and inconsistency detection

## Code Example
```javascript
function solveHorn(rules, facts){
  const trueSet = new Set(facts);
  let queue = rules.filter(r => r.antecedent.every(v => trueSet.has(v)));
  while (queue.length){
    const r = queue.shift();
    if (!trueSet.has(r.consequent)){
      trueSet.add(r.consequent);
      queue = rules.filter(x => x.antecedent.every(v => trueSet.has(v)) && !trueSet.has(x.consequent));
    }
  }
  return trueSet;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to non-Horn clauses?
- How would you handle incremental updates when facts change?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 3-valued semantics in robotics sensor fusion and implement a concrete propagator for arbitrary rule sets.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic (T>U>F)
- Propagation rules for ¬, ∧, ∨, and → (X→Y ≡ ¬X ∨ Y)
- Backtracking to avoid F while preserving at least one U

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple 3-valued implication using Kleene logic
function implies(x, y){
  const not = v => v === 'T' ? 'F' : v === 'F' ? 'T' : 'U';
  const or = (a,b) => (a==='T' || b==='T') ? 'T' : (a==='U' || b==='U') ? 'U' : 'F';
  return or(not(x), y);
}
```

