## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to reason about combined constraints and partial knowledge in a policy graph using three-valued logic; requires identifying a concrete satisfying assignment and developing a general propagation strategy for 3-valued logic with conjunction and implication operations.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene 3-valued logic semantics for implication (→), conjunction (∧), and disjunction (∨)
- Propagation of unknown (U) values through mixed logical operators
- Fixpoint computation with backtracking to avoid false (F) while preserving unknowns

## Code Example

```javascript
function imp(a,b){ // a->b in Kleene logic
  const not = v => v==='T'?'F': v==='F'?'T':'U';
  const or  = (x,y) => (x==='T' || y==='T') ? 'T' : (x==='U' || y==='U') ? 'U' : 'F';
  return or(not(a), b);
}

function propagate(rules, values){
  let changed;
  do{
    changed = false;
    for(const [name, expr] of rules){
      const newVal = expr(values);
      if(values[name] !== newVal){
        values[name] = newVal;
        changed = true;
      }
    }
  } while(changed);
  return values;
}
```

The algorithm iteratively propagates known values through constraints until a fixpoint is reached, ensuring no constraint evaluates to F while maintaining at least one U value.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests design of tri-valued constraint systems with a custom operator and backtracking propagation, ensuring candidates reason about Kleene semantics and propagation rules.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic with T > U > F; ∧/∨ act as min/max.
- Custom operator Nand defined as not(and(x,y)).
- Backtracking propagation to prune domains and find a satisfying assignment.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Basic tri-valued lattice: T > U > F
const rank = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
function not(x){ return x==='T'?'F': x==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function and(a,b){ return rank[a] <= rank[b] ? a : b; }
function or(a,b){ return rank[a] >= rank[b] ? a : b; }
function nand(a,b){ return not(and(a,b)); }
function implies(p,q){ return or(not(p), q); }

// sample assignment
let F1='T', F2='F', F3='U', F4='F';
console.log(implies(or(F1,F2), F3)); // U
console.log(and(F2,F4)); // F
console.log(nand(F3,F4)); // T
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to N flags and arbitrary constraint sets?
- What changes when moving to a 4-valued logic or alternative lattices?

